{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763387Z","id":"1a9284c6b70d6ece70e6d3cd5b1dcb796c4cd266cacb2ecc0d976541cd2daf9b","body":{"detail":"Every satellite crate (toolpath-convo, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-dot, toolpath-claude, path-cli, ...) takes `toolpath = { workspace = true }` and their published manifests on crates.io require `toolpath ^0.7.0`. This change moves the workspace entry to 0.8.0 and edits production code in several of them (crates/toolpath-convo/src/derive.rs, crates/toolpath-git/src/lib.rs, crates/toolpath-github/src/lib.rs, crates/toolpath-md/src/source/mod.rs add `parent: None`), yet their versions, CHANGELOG entries and site/_data/crates.json rows are unchanged. scripts/release.sh runs scripts/check-registry-compat.py before publishing; against this branch it fails:\n\n    toolpath-convo 0.11.1: INCOMPATIBLE  toolpath ^0.7.0 (registry) vs 0.8.0 (workspace)\n    toolpath-git 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE ...  toolpath-github 0.6.0 ... toolpath-md 0.7.0 ... toolpath-dot 0.5.0 ...\n    registry compatibility check failed for: toolpath-convo, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-dot\n\nThe script's own explanation (scripts/release.sh:173-185) is that publishing toolpath 0.8.0 alone leaves consumers such as pathbase (which pins `toolpath = \"0.7\"` and `toolpath-convo = \"0.11\"`) with two toolpath majors in one graph and E0308 type mismatches. CLAUDE.md's release checklist (\"Versioning and release checklist\") requires a bump whenever a crate's dependencies change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":27},"end":{"line":27},"content_hash":"cfdd8cf41c8921e6f4831ae1ddcb346e55ba0a21a844a404c7653512fd45bd8a"},"suggested_fix":"Bump every workspace crate that depends on toolpath (patch is enough for the satellites; path-cli per its own rule), add their CHANGELOG lines and update site/_data/crates.json, then re-run `scripts/release.sh` (dry run) until the registry compatibility check passes.","summary":"toolpath is bumped to 0.8.0 but no crate that depends on it is bumped, so scripts/release.sh refuses to publish this workspace","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763445Z","id":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","body":{"detail":"RFC.md \"Toolpath Base (deprecated)\" (RFC.md:663-678) now says the `toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id>` base form is deprecated and \"Writers MUST use path.parent instead\", and crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:532 marks `Base::toolpath` `#[deprecated]`. FAQ.md:40-42 (\"Can I use Toolpath without a VCS?\") still answers: \"A path's `base` can use a `toolpath:` URI to branch from another path's step, creating a pure Toolpath chain with no VCS backing.\" The FAQ is published at /faq/ (site/pages/faq.md:41 carries the same sentence; site/eleventy.config.js:95 rewrites the RFC's FAQ link to it), so the site contradicts the RFC it links to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":40},"end":{"line":42},"content_hash":"81037cd0b3434b5331de613cedb60bf29dfdea1cec2ed45283b7b23f9479c1c7"},"suggested_fix":"Rewrite the FAQ answer around `path.parent` (continuation/fork from a step in another document) and `file:///` bases; regenerate site/pages/faq.md if it is derived.","summary":"FAQ still tells writers to use the toolpath: base URI that this change deprecates","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763513Z","id":"89687a0457f96d18da9369221151f1321147764ba88459a06804ca5f4c3ef041","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:432-434: \"the branch merged at step m starts at m.parents[1..] and follows parents[0] until it reaches a step on m's own first-parent chain.\" Read literally, the step on m's chain is reached and so included. The design document this section implements, docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:119-121, states the same rule with \"exclusive\", and its own example (agent-call ... agent-result) only works if the fork step `agent-call` is not part of the subagent branch. Two readers implementing `branches/{step_id}` from the RFC alone can disagree on whether the fork step is in the branch.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":432},"end":{"line":434},"content_hash":"5bb687b3795cc39b2857e05040691a1a9b7791c26868faaddf4d7dc141d429b5"},"suggested_fix":"Add the word: \"... until it reaches a step on m's own first-parent chain, exclusive (the fork step is on the main line, not in the branch)\".","summary":"The branch-slicing rule does not say whether the first-parent-chain step it stops at belongs to the branch","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763554Z","id":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:693 (\"names the step in another, frozen path\") and RFC.md:725 (\"resumed after the parent path froze\") introduce a lifecycle state the format RFC does not define anywhere (`grep -n frozen RFC.md` finds only these two lines). The same wording is in crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:152, crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:362 and CHANGELOG.md:10. Freezing is a Pathbase server behaviour (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:184-185: \"The server owns freezing\"); a document with `parent` is well-formed whether or not any store has frozen the parent, so a reader of the RFC cannot tell what, if anything, they must check.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":693},"end":{"line":694},"content_hash":"058207162042804526d5587cd472f2f7b45362c5955aee9be9442f45cf1d007a"},"suggested_fix":"Either define the term once (e.g. in Vocabulary: a path a store no longer accepts steps for) and say it is a store-level condition the format does not verify, or drop \"frozen\" from the format text and describe the relation as \"the same work, resumed after the parent path ended\".","summary":"Path Parent requires the parent path to be \"frozen\" but the RFC never defines frozen","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763594Z","id":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:72 defines base as \"The root context (repo, commit) a path branches from\", but RFC.md:628-631 and RFC.md:622 now define it as \"the repository or directory state the work ran against\" and move lineage to `path.parent`. RFC.md:77 defines fork as \"Where paths diverge\", while RFC.md:726 uses `fork` as a `parent.relation` value meaning new work rooted at an older step of another path, and RFC.md:437 calls the tool-use step inside one path the \"fork point\". The table is the first thing a reader meets; the new `parent`/`continuation` rows were added but the two rows they interact with were not revised.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":72},"end":{"line":77},"content_hash":"1d07d4670812f6b1e88925158411b709039ff43772c104aa8d90eb842b724628"},"suggested_fix":"Reword `base` to the \"ran against\" definition and either give `fork` both senses (a divergence point within a path; a `parent.relation` naming new work rooted at an older step of another path) or rename the vocabulary entry to \"fork point\".","summary":"Vocabulary rows for base and fork no longer match the meanings the rest of the RFC gives them","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763632Z","id":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:665-666 keeps the old meaning (\"names a step in another path within the same graph\") and RFC.md:676-678 says \"Writers MUST use path.parent instead\". Two gaps: (1) `path.parent.uri` is required and is \"The document holding the parent path\" (RFC.md:717); nothing says what a writer puts there when the parent path is in the same document, so the one case the deprecated form handled has no stated replacement. (2) Nothing says what a reader does with a document that still carries `base.uri: toolpath:...` (treat it as an opaque base with no lineage? synthesise a `parent` with which `relation`?). Existing documents and `Base::toolpath` callers (crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:532) will keep producing it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":663},"end":{"line":678},"content_hash":"db9827e8c415180a53c7003cb508d0cdbb3e32f46bed81bd5d37b0445909d9ac"},"suggested_fix":"Add two sentences: readers MUST treat a `toolpath:` base as an opaque base URI and MUST NOT infer lineage from it; and for a parent in the same document, writers use the document's own URI (or `graph_ref`) in `parent.uri`.","summary":"The deprecated Toolpath Base section neither gives readers a rule for existing toolpath: bases nor a path.parent form for the same-graph case it covered","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925223Z","id":"56d5234d918dee93d910a3fa963452af5c4c26e90e81298d514b7c7ab2e0aa61","body":{"detail":"Cargo.toml:27 and crates/toolpath/Cargo.toml:3 move toolpath to 0.8.0; no other crates/*/Cargo.toml version changes in `git diff origin/main...HEAD`. Running the check scripts/release.sh:201 invokes, with every crates/*/Cargo.toml name=version and all satellites in --skip:\n\n    python3 scripts/check-registry-compat.py path-cli=0.29.0 ... toolpath=0.8.0 --skip toolpath-codex toolpath-convo ...\n    toolpath-codex 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n      toolpath ^0.7.0 (registry) vs 0.8.0 (workspace)\n    toolpath-convo 0.11.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-copilot 0.1.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-cursor 0.2.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-dot 0.5.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-gemini 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-git 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-github 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-md 0.7.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-opencode 0.5.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-pi 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    registry compatibility check failed for: toolpath-codex, toolpath-convo, toolpath-copilot, toolpath-cursor, toolpath-dot, toolpath-gemini, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-opencode, toolpath-pi\n    exit=1\n\nThe affected set is wider than the finding lists: toolpath-codex, toolpath-copilot, toolpath-cursor, toolpath-gemini, toolpath-opencode and toolpath-pi fail too. CLAUDE.md:166-174 (\"Versioning and release checklist\") requires crate Cargo.toml, workspace Cargo.toml and site/_data/crates.json to move together on a bump. `concern` is the right kind: nothing fails before a release is attempted, but scripts/release.sh will refuse to publish this workspace as-is.","kind":"comment","references":"1a9284c6b70d6ece70e6d3cd5b1dcb796c4cd266cacb2ecc0d976541cd2daf9b","summary":"confirmed: the registry compatibility check fails for every satellite crate that depends on toolpath","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925253Z","id":"cda1def350a93147203432ef6b42514a3885e59089cf51b38a6b6a8028e0c8bf","body":{"detail":"FAQ.md:40-42: \"A path's `base` can use a `toolpath:` URI to branch from another path's step, creating a pure Toolpath chain with no VCS backing.\" site/pages/faq.md:41 carries the same sentence. Neither file is in `git diff origin/main...HEAD --stat`. RFC.md:663-678 (\"Toolpath Base (deprecated)\"): \"This form is deprecated. ... Writers MUST use `path.parent` instead.\" crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:530-533 marks `Base::toolpath` `#[deprecated(since = \"0.8.0\", note = \"name the step in `PathIdentity::parent` instead\")]`. site/eleventy.config.js:95 rewrites `(./FAQ.md)` links in the RFC to `(/faq/)`, so the published RFC links to a FAQ that contradicts it. `concern` fits: published documentation now instructs writers to do what the spec forbids.","kind":"comment","references":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","summary":"confirmed: FAQ.md:40-42 and site/pages/faq.md:41 still recommend the toolpath: base URI the RFC now deprecates","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925267Z","id":"7006e74fb003b29ebe8934e70e1a5bf25e7462e7cb648567a949a22f63cb49e5","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:72: `| **base** | The root context (repo, commit) a path branches from|`. RFC.md:622: \"**base**: The repository state the work ran against (repo + ref + branch)\"; RFC.md:628-631: \"anchors the path to the repository or directory state the work ran against ... Lineage between paths is a different fact and lives in `path.parent`.\" RFC.md:77: `| **fork** | Where paths diverge |`; RFC.md:726 defines `relation: fork` as \"New work rooted at an older step of the parent path\"; RFC.md:436-437 calls the tool-use step inside a path \"the fork point\". The new `parent` and `continuation` rows (RFC.md:74-75) are in the diff; rows 72 and 77 are not. `suggestion` is the right kind: a terminology inconsistency, not a defect in the format.","kind":"comment","references":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","summary":"confirmed: the vocabulary rows for base and fork were not revised to match the new body text","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925277Z","id":"66224b613e2a9f70a99a61a21d17bb4499b4996805beba7d1ef8d20f50e35913","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:432-434: \"the branch merged at step *m* starts at `m.parents[1..]` and follows `parents[0]` until it reaches a step on *m*'s own first-parent chain.\" docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:119-121: \"start at `m.parents[1..]`, walk `parents[0]` until reaching a step on `m`'s own first-parent chain, exclusive.\" The RFC example at RFC.md:436-437 makes the tool-use step the fork point on the main line, which only matches the design document's exclusive reading. `suggestion` fits: one missing word in normative prose, with the intended meaning recoverable from the example.","kind":"comment","references":"89687a0457f96d18da9369221151f1321147764ba88459a06804ca5f4c3ef041","summary":"confirmed: RFC.md:432-434 omits the \"exclusive\" the design document states for the branch slice","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925286Z","id":"115b52188daa9d6a9c12677707c7e419294fa6f8745146215407adf064a5c76c","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:665-666 still defines the form as naming \"a step in another path within the same graph\"; RFC.md:676-678 says only \"This form is deprecated. ... Writers MUST use `path.parent` instead.\" No sentence tells a reader how to treat a document carrying `base.uri: toolpath:...` (`grep -n 'toolpath:' RFC.md` -> lines 196, 665, 671, 761, 785; none is a reader rule). RFC.md:717 defines `parent.uri` as \"The document holding the parent path. Same URI forms as `$ref`, no fragment\" and RFC.md:783-785 lists those forms as URLs, `file:///` and `toolpath://archive-name/path-id`; `grep -n -i 'same document' RFC.md` returns nothing, so the same-graph case has no stated `parent.uri` value. crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:530-540 keeps `Base::toolpath` producing the form. `suggestion` is right: a specification gap, not a failing behaviour.","kind":"comment","references":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","summary":"confirmed: the deprecated section gives neither a reader rule for existing toolpath: bases nor a parent.uri form for the same-document case","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925296Z","id":"21145918c65df4cde9ccb836126cf7184471a0f876368c6c91ed1dc5ee5ff74e","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:693: \"`path.parent` names the step in another, frozen path that this path continues from.\" RFC.md:725: \"The same work, resumed after the parent path froze.\" `grep -n -i frozen RFC.md` returns only line 693 (line 725 uses \"froze\"); the Vocabulary table RFC.md:68-77 has no entry for it. The same wording is in crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:150-152, crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:362 and CHANGELOG.md:10. Freezing is defined only as a Pathbase server behaviour in docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:184-185 (\"The server owns freezing\"). `suggestion` fits: an undefined term in the spec, with nothing for a format reader to verify.","kind":"comment","references":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","summary":"confirmed: the RFC uses frozen/froze in Path Parent without defining the term anywhere","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T17:41:56.078808Z","id":"221e2fe784e4f77d5cad8e77e78902e2001fb2e717cc18f3515a4d6687b654f3","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","summary":"Decision (project owner, 2026-10-07): the toolpath: base form is not deprecated. base records the artifact state the work started from; for a project whose state is tracked in Toolpath rather than git that state is a step, so toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id> is the Toolpath analogue of github:org/repo + ref. parent records which earlier work this path continues; the two answer different questions and a path may carry both. Fix: RFC.md restores the Toolpath Base section (no deprecation, the no-VCS rationale back), removes the 'Writers MUST use path.parent' sentence, and the Base Context intro and Path Parent section say the two coexist; Base::toolpath loses #[deprecated]; schema base.uri description and CHANGELOG follow. FAQ.md:40-42 then needs only a wording change: the base is the artifact state at another path's step, not a branch of its conversation. Design doc updated accordingly (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md, Summary).","tags":["decision","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T17:41:56.121512Z","id":"db99daed51465319a7236c68d3130ff8fefbea74c9145855e00a6563a78f6719","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","summary":"Resolved by the decision on d75fa1ac: the toolpath: base form stays, so readers handle existing toolpath: bases exactly as before and no reader rule for a deprecated form is needed. The same-document case is not parent's job: branching from a step inside the same path is step.parents; a path whose artifacts start at the state produced by a step elsewhere is base.uri = toolpath:<path>/<step>; parent is reserved for a continuation or fork of work in another document, so parent.uri is correctly required. The fix for d75fa1ac should state this three-way split in the Path Parent section.","tags":["decision","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T17:54:34.328515Z","id":"f5da93680b85f77fe0877efd479556ebc95b30e89ed3aa8306890248140ab183","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","summary":"Decision (project owner, 2026-10-07): path.parent carries no relation field. Parent is { uri, path, step }; 'continuation' (parent.step is the parent path's head) and 'fork' (any other step) are derived terms, several children may share a head and all are continuations, and which one a viewer follows is outside the format. So the vocabulary rows become: base = the artifact state the work started from (see d75fa1ac); parent = the step in another path a path descends from; continuation and fork defined structurally as above, with fork no longer colliding with an enum value. Fix lands with the relation removal in RFC.md (Path Parent, First Parent, vocabulary), types.rs, the schema, the example and RFC-jsonl.","tags":["decision","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:01:51.980578Z","id":"ce96a022044bec1176adea89e31fcedc9ed3c9a3cb6a85444e29903f4e699b81","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","summary":"Decision (project owner, 2026-10-07): the branch slice is inclusive of the fork step. Walking parents[0] from the merge's non-first parents stops at the first step on the merge's own first-parent chain and that step is part of the branch: it is the tool-use step that spawned the subagent and carries what the subagent was told to do. Design doc updated (First Parent bullet and the branches route row); RFC.md First Parent must say the same.","tags":["decision","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.547784Z","id":"ed9af24d4c2122117169234421dcfff17862178e9f0a61604ff3c2c9a40a70dc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:ae10ceb","summary":"Minor bumps for every published crate whose registry manifest requires toolpath ^0.7: crates/toolpath-{convo,git,github,dot,md,gemini,codex,copilot,opencode,cursor,pi}/Cargo.toml, Cargo.toml [workspace.dependencies], site/_data/crates.json, CHANGELOG.md toolpath 0.8.0 entry. scripts/check-registry-compat.py run as release.sh runs it (--skip pathbase-client, the only crate still on the registry at its local version) exits 0.","supersedes":"1a9284c6b70d6ece70e6d3cd5b1dcb796c4cd266cacb2ecc0d976541cd2daf9b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.596404Z","id":"b9d298e149d0bfa0882b94f685e95a58a656a662de49ad43012fed20340e08e7","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:1ece557","summary":"Decision applied: the toolpath: base form is not deprecated. RFC.md 'Toolpath Base' (RFC.md:666-687) restores the no-VCS rationale and the github:org/repo + ref analogue; 'Writers MUST use path.parent' is gone; Base Context intro (RFC.md:632-635) and the Path Parent closing paragraph (RFC.md:748-755) say base and parent coexist and state the step.parents / base / parent split. crates/toolpath/src/types.rs Base::toolpath has no #[deprecated], test_base_toolpath_constructor has no #[allow(deprecated)]; schema base.uri description and examples keep toolpath:path-main/step-005; CHANGELOG.md follows. FAQ.md:40-44 and site/pages/faq.md:41 describe the base as the artifact state at another path's step and point lineage at path.parent.","supersedes":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.645184Z","id":"ba73996ea1a212bf102ccc6be04994387682107272fb4e467205c88dbd6c1814","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:1ece557","summary":"Settled by the same change as d75fa1ac: the toolpath: base form stays (RFC.md 'Toolpath Base', RFC.md:666-687), so readers handle existing toolpath: bases as before and no reader rule for a deprecated form is needed. The same-document case is not parent's job: RFC.md:748-755 states the three-way split (step.parents inside a path; base, toolpath: form included, for the artifact state; parent for descent from work in another document), so parent.uri stays required. 'frozen' is gone from RFC.md (grep -c frozen RFC.md = 0), crates/toolpath/src/types.rs, the schema and CHANGELOG.md as of git:6035c22.","supersedes":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.692019Z","id":"23394efc4a3f2ef0d0cd0ba48cbc65742049d9377f6786cef3fda655761cfafc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:6035c22","summary":"Decision applied: Parent is { uri, path, step } with no relation (crates/toolpath/src/types.rs Parent, Parent::new; Relation enum and Parent::continuation/fork removed; schema parent.required = [uri, path, step]; examples/path-05-continuation.path.json and .jsonl; docs/RFC-jsonl.md:180). RFC.md Path Parent (RFC.md:700-755) defines continuation (parent.step is the parent's head) and fork (any other step) structurally, says several children of one head are all continuations and the choice is a viewer's. Vocabulary rows RFC.md:67-78 for base, parent, continuation and fork follow (git:5bd526c). Tests: test_parent_roundtrip, test_parent_fields_required, test_path_with_parent_roundtrip, jsonl roundtrip_parent.","supersedes":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.789457Z","id":"5f73035e593a9553db4a075953809da3e93eb809ecd39413e490459a4adaeb31","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:12bd337","summary":"Decision applied: RFC.md First Parent (RFC.md:434-437) says the step the walk stops at on the merge's first-parent chain is part of the branch, and that for a delegation it is the tool-use step that spawned the subagent and carries what it was told to do. Vocabulary rows RFC.md:72 (base) and RFC.md:75 (fork) revised in git:5bd526c; the in-path branch point is no longer called a fork point (RFC.md:439-441).","supersedes":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T18:21:05.835571Z","id":"b3be56699792e42a50aaa09d53db83511d759eb0b70e168821ace7a50ae87e66","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:12bd337","summary":"RFC.md First Parent (RFC.md:434-437) now says the branch slice includes the step it stops at (inclusive, per the owner's decision recorded on 8faa873b and the updated design doc), so two implementers of branches/{step_id} agree. 'frozen' is gone from RFC.md, crates/toolpath/src/types.rs, crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json and CHANGELOG.md (git:6035c22); RFC.md:742-744 says a reader classifies against the parent's head as it stands and the parent document is expected not to change.","supersedes":"89687a0457f96d18da9369221151f1321147764ba88459a06804ca5f4c3ef041","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:10:59.563749Z","id":"0998ef04a0e2b860b14e3aa84e49fdc9bb6a5ce34e15587a523cefc79cc5466e","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:141-143 now reads 'Cross-path step references are not supported; the one link between paths is path.parent, which names a step in another document explicitly.' The same RFC keeps the Toolpath Base form (RFC.md:666-687), where base.uri = 'toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id>' names a step of another path, and the Path Parent section (RFC.md:749-753) lists it as one of three distinct mechanisms. The two passages disagree about whether parent is the only cross-path reference.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":141},"end":{"line":143},"content_hash":"35ce99570332659ac840dc532511305ba6c8c8f98b4940b21740d196638dfc31"},"suggested_fix":"Reword RFC.md:141-143 to say step references in parents and head resolve within the containing path, and that the cross-path references are path.parent (lineage) and a toolpath: base (starting artifact state), linking to both sections.","summary":"The ID Uniqueness section says path.parent is 'the one link between paths', but the toolpath: base also names a step in another path","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:10:59.563786Z","id":"3ed11145288df5161b276d403eb5ec937c5c9bc4e2dc38cfc9f0f1ed91289acf","body":{"detail":"Before this change the section opened 'For paths branching from a step in another path (within the same graph)'. The rewrite (RFC.md:668-670) removed that scope and replaced it with 'For a project whose artifacts are tracked in Toolpath rather than in a VCS'. The toolpath: base URI carries no document locator, unlike parent.uri, and RFC.md:140 says paths in different graphs may share an id. So a reader now has no rule for resolving path-main in 'toolpath:path-main/step-005', and FAQ.md:40-42 describes it as naming 'another path's step' with no scope either.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":666},"end":{"line":681},"content_hash":"d1c06acae8781ea5f97cb49064d221cb2f2e205487791c58279f8d9ebe091a56"},"suggested_fix":"Restore the scope in RFC.md Toolpath Base: the path id resolves among the paths of the containing graph. If cross-document starting states are meant to be supported, say they are not expressible with toolpath: and point to the intended form.","summary":"The Toolpath Base rewrite dropped 'within the same graph', so the RFC no longer says where the path id in toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id> is looked up","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:10:59.563818Z","id":"8439787802aa8bcc1912ccb08ac478b8452d5d07754db02ad26fe4267f794147","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:102-104 says a bundle of related agent sessions is one Graph with several paths, and parent.uri is required (RFC.md:727). Running `path p merge examples/path-02-local-session.path.json examples/path-05-continuation.path.json` gives one graph holding both path-session-xyz and path-session-xyz-2. The child's parent.uri still points at file:///home/alex/.toolpath/path-02-local-session.path.json, while the parent path sits next to it in the same document. The Path Parent section does not say whether a reader should first match parent.path against sibling paths, whether parent.uri may name the containing document, or what a writer bundling both paths should put in uri. Readers will pick different answers: some resolve the sibling, some fetch the file, and some report a dangling parent.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":722},"end":{"line":726},"content_hash":"3e559665c819fb4ba3f2f3f2d2f3b4ecac4a40c36d1cb837977616c0d804f169"},"suggested_fix":"Add a rule to RFC.md Path Parent, for example: 'If the containing graph has an inline path whose id equals parent.path and whose steps include parent.step, readers resolve to it; parent.uri records where the parent was first published.' Or say that bundling a parent and child in one graph is unsupported. Either way, give `path p merge` a defined result.","summary":"The RFC does not say how parent resolves when the parent path is a sibling in the same graph, which `path p merge` produces from the shipped examples","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:10:59.563849Z","id":"0b85baf0e444d8651107f693ec4b2d10227fb56ea9ae032b9e4d99fd29c474c2","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:724 says parent.uri takes 'Same URI forms as $ref'. RFC.md:124-125 says Graph $ref entries MUST point to sealed .path.json files, not .path.jsonl streams, and RFC.md:746-747 expects the parent document not to change. Read together, a live .path.jsonl session (for example the files `path p track` writes, crates/path-cli/src/cmd_track.rs:342) cannot be a parent until it is exported. But neither the Path Parent section nor docs/RFC-jsonl.md (which adds parent to PathOpen at lines 180 and 196) says so, and the design doc's '.path.json' qualifier (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:50) did not make it into the RFC. A writer resuming a tracked session will reasonably point uri at the .path.jsonl file.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":724},"end":{"line":747},"content_hash":"92513269965fbc37b489c62494d7d24b92812b075498bc430964d093e5d745b0"},"suggested_fix":"State the rule in RFC.md Path Parent, e.g. 'parent.uri MUST name a sealed .path.json document (or a server URL serving one), not a .path.jsonl stream', and repeat it in docs/RFC-jsonl.md next to the PathOpen parent row.","summary":"Path Parent never says whether parent.uri may name a .path.jsonl stream, although resumed sessions often come from one","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:11:35.270783Z","id":"f6278ebca3f3fbd50c70bc6ea74dc4b6c0ae25d32bba6174435181ae2975edcb","body":{"detail":"Cargo.toml:30 and crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml:3 keep toolpath-claude at 0.13.4, and the CHANGELOG.md:26-31 list of cascading minor bumps leaves it out. toolpath-claude takes `toolpath = { workspace = true }` (crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml:16), so this change moves it to toolpath 0.8, and `toolpath_claude::derive_path` / `derive_project` (crates/toolpath-claude/src/derive.rs:25,45) return `toolpath::v1::Path`. 0.13.4 is not yet on crates.io (registry has 0.13.3; `scripts/check-registry-compat.py` reports 404 for it), which is why the release script's registry check passes: that check only inspects crates already published at their local version. But publishing 0.13.4 with a toolpath ^0.8 dependency is a breaking change shipped in the compatible slot: a downstream crate depending on `toolpath = \"0.7\"` and `toolpath-claude = \"0.13\"` picks up 0.13.4 on `cargo update` and gets a `toolpath 0.8` Path where it expects a 0.7 one (E0308, two toolpath majors in one graph -- the failure scripts/release.sh:173-185 describes). CLAUDE.md \"Versioning and release checklist\" reserves the patch slot for fixes and additive changes. Every other toolpath dependent got a minor bump for exactly this reason. Separately, the same CHANGELOG paragraph says the cascaded crates have \"no source changes of their own\", but toolpath-convo, toolpath-git and toolpath-github each gain `parent: None` in production code (crates/toolpath-convo/src/derive.rs:472, crates/toolpath-git/src/lib.rs:248, crates/toolpath-github/src/lib.rs:535); behaviour is unchanged, so 'no behaviour change' is the accurate phrase.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":30},"end":{"line":30},"content_hash":"b5277df405af6bf3385aadfac719bb97a1c11a2d43b36fd67bbe89f321d76020"},"suggested_fix":"Bump toolpath-claude to 0.14.0 (folding the unreleased 0.13.4 entry into it) in crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml, Cargo.toml [workspace.dependencies], site/_data/crates.json and Cargo.lock, add it to the CHANGELOG.md cascade list, and reword 'no source changes of their own' to 'no behaviour change'.","summary":"toolpath-claude stays at 0.13.4, a patch version, although its toolpath dependency moves from 0.7 to 0.8 and its public API returns toolpath types","tags":["review","review:pr-354-qual-2","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.169016Z","id":"6ef49da24ca8c0e2f6f34a30480a7bda3803168d97faa098887152ba3db936b2","body":{"detail":"This change added the sentence at RFC.md:144-145: 'Cross-path step references are not supported; the one link between paths is path.parent' (git diff origin/main...ae10ceb -- RFC.md, hunk @@ -139,7 +141,8 @@). RFC.md:681 keeps 'toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id> - the artifact state produced by a step in a path', and RFC.md:750-752 lists base, including the toolpath: form, as a separate mechanism from parent. Under the owner decision on d75fa1ac, the toolpath: base is a starting state and not lineage, so it can fairly be called 'not a link'. It is still a cross-path step reference, which the new sentence says is not supported. Suggestion is the right kind: the two passages disagree in wording only, and the format behaves the same either way.","kind":"comment","references":"0998ef04a0e2b860b14e3aa84e49fdc9bb6a5ce34e15587a523cefc79cc5466e","summary":"confirmed: RFC.md:143-145 says path.parent is the one link between paths and cross-path step references are unsupported, but the toolpath: base at RFC.md:666-687 names a step in another path","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.169039Z","id":"5e39b560bc62abe181255e4bfb35a8afa99d20bd4f5d52adb4a8ed08acf9e06a","body":{"detail":"git diff origin/main...ae10ceb -- RFC.md, hunk @@ -628,7 +665,9 @@, removes 'For paths branching from a step in another path (within the same graph):' and adds 'For a project whose artifacts are tracked in Toolpath rather than in a VCS, the state the work started from is a step' (RFC.md:668-670). The toolpath: URI form at RFC.md:681 has no document locator. RFC.md:140 allows paths in different graphs to share an id. Nothing else in RFC.md says where the path id is looked up (grep -n 'toolpath:' RFC.md). The owner decision on 59d1f188 (reply db99daed) uses the toolpath: base for the case parent does not cover, since parent is reserved for 'work in another document'. That decision implies the same-graph scope the rewrite dropped, so restoring the scope agrees with it. Concern is the right kind: this is an ambiguity in the spec, and no code failure follows from it yet.","kind":"comment","references":"3ed11145288df5161b276d403eb5ec937c5c9bc4e2dc38cfc9f0f1ed91289acf","summary":"confirmed: the rewrite removed '(within the same graph)' from Toolpath Base, so the RFC no longer says where the path id in a toolpath: URI resolves","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.169055Z","id":"d45e20de3ec45d3d513fc8acfd02f7d84e67769d73afd48584a88a7ab724b49f","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:724 defines parent.uri as 'The document holding the parent path', and all three fields are required (RFC.md:728). After `path p merge`, the child's parent.uri still names file:///home/alex/.toolpath/path-02-local-session.path.json. A reader resolves the parent by fetching that document, which is a defined rule, not a gap. The owner decision on thread 59d1f188 (reply db99daed, 2026-10-07) says: 'The same-document case is not parent's job ... parent is reserved for a continuation or fork of work in another document, so parent.uri is correctly required.' RFC.md:752-753 says the same: 'descent from work in another document is parent'. The finding asks for a sibling-first rule or a same-document uri form. Both reopen that decision.","kind":"comment","references":"8439787802aa8bcc1912ccb08ac478b8452d5d07754db02ad26fe4267f794147","summary":"refuted: the owner settled that parent is for descent from work in another document, and parent.uri names that document, so a merged bundle has a defined resolution","tags":["verified:refuted","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.169071Z","id":"88cf2b7831b84f0c3d879c693fff0b88d4176d570996b7aa2c471dafd085cecc","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:724 says only 'Same URI forms as $ref, no fragment'. RFC.md:124-125 says 'Graph $ref entries MUST point to sealed .path.json files, not to .path.jsonl streams'. That rule is written about Graph $ref entries, and Path Parent does not say it applies to parent.uri. docs/RFC-jsonl.md:180 and :196 add parent to PathOpen without mentioning the restriction. The design doc does narrow it: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:50 lists 'an s3:// or file:// .path.json'. crates/path-cli/src/cmd_track.rs:342 writes live sessions as '{session_id}.path.jsonl', which is what a resume would point at. Suggestion is the right kind: this is a gap in the spec text.","kind":"comment","references":"0b85baf0e444d8651107f693ec4b2d10227fb56ea9ae032b9e4d99fd29c474c2","summary":"confirmed: Path Parent never says whether parent.uri may name a .path.jsonl stream, and readers have to infer it from the $ref rule","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:02.179419Z","id":"edc856e16aaf74e4fa8d06773edf0accc87441c0d686fe333b22aa28c210b449","body":{"detail":"Cargo.toml:30 and crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml:3 are 0.13.4 (same as origin/main); crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml:16 takes toolpath = { workspace = true }, now 0.8.0. crates.io API (https://crates.io/api/v1/crates/toolpath-claude) lists 0.13.3 as the newest version, and 0.13.3's dependencies require toolpath ^0.7.1, so 0.13.4 would be the first ^0.8 release in a 0.13 semver-compatible slot. crates/toolpath-claude/src/derive.rs:13,25,45 return toolpath::v1::Path in the public API. CHANGELOG.md:26-31 cascade list omits toolpath-claude. 'no source changes of their own' is inaccurate: crates/toolpath-convo/src/derive.rs:472, crates/toolpath-git/src/lib.rs:248, crates/toolpath-github/src/lib.rs:535 each add parent: None. concern is right: the defect surfaces at publish, not before merge.","kind":"comment","references":"f6278ebca3f3fbd50c70bc6ea74dc4b6c0ae25d32bba6174435181ae2975edcb","summary":"confirmed: toolpath-claude stays at unreleased patch 0.13.4 while its toolpath dependency moves 0.7 -> 0.8","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T14:14:44.190203Z","id":"6bdb0ce4926dffbb9a33b1c70cca6b8e3eaabafb968ddb9b70423d85af4d316d","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Refuted by reply d45e20de3ec45d3d513fc8acfd02f7d84e67769d73afd48584a88a7ab724b49f: the owner decision on 59d1f188 (reply db99daed) reserves parent for descent from work in another document, so parent.uri names that document and a merged bundle resolves by fetching it.","supersedes":"8439787802aa8bcc1912ccb08ac478b8452d5d07754db02ad26fe4267f794147","tags":["reason:invalid","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-13T18:52:23.889989Z","id":"46b127997b593e2742541d60a9b6e9f3dc0de5578dc244d720d7067b309584bf","body":{"detail":"Owner approval, 2026-10-13. A crate that now depends on toolpath 0.8 and returns 0.8 types gets a minor bump, not a patch, and the CHANGELOG lists every cascaded crate honestly, including the three that gained parent: None in production code. Part of the #354 merge gate.","kind":"decision","references":"f6278ebca3f3fbd50c70bc6ea74dc4b6c0ae25d32bba6174435181ae2975edcb","suggested_fix":"Bump toolpath-claude to 0.14.0 (folding the unreleased 0.13.4 entry into it) in crates/toolpath-claude/Cargo.toml, Cargo.toml [workspace.dependencies], site/_data/crates.json and Cargo.lock, add it to the CHANGELOG.md cascade list, and reword 'no source changes of their own' to 'no behaviour change'.","summary":"Decided: fix as suggested; blocking for the 0.8.0 release","tags":["review:pr-354-qual-2","decision","session:claude-code:2b4a790f-c163-4549-93fa-572ef111cb5e"]}}
